Here's a discriminated-union approach. Each status carries only the data that makes sense for it, and the compiler checks that every status is handled.

```typescript
// ---------- Shared types ----------

type OrderId = string;
type Cents = number; // store money as integer cents to avoid float issues

interface LineItem {
  sku: string;
  name: string;
  quantity: number;
  unitPrice: Cents;
}

interface OrderBase {
  id: OrderId;
  customerId: string;
  items: LineItem[];
  total: Cents;
  createdAt: Date;
}

// ---------- Status-specific variants ----------

interface PendingOrder extends OrderBase {
  status: "pending";
}

interface PaidOrder extends OrderBase {
  status: "paid";
  paidAt: Date;
  paymentId: string;
}

interface ShippedOrder extends OrderBase {
  status: "shipped";
  paidAt: Date;
  paymentId: string;
  shippedAt: Date;
  carrier: string;
  trackingNumber: string;
}

interface RefundedOrder extends OrderBase {
  status: "refunded";
  paidAt: Date;
  paymentId: string;
  refundedAt: Date;
  refundAmount: Cents;
  reason?: string;
}

// ---------- The union ----------

type Order = PendingOrder | PaidOrder | ShippedOrder | RefundedOrder;

type OrderStatus = Order["status"]; // "pending" | "paid" | "shipped" | "refunded"

// ---------- Exhaustiveness helper ----------

function assertNever(value: never): never {
  throw new Error(`Unhandled order status: ${JSON.stringify(value)}`);
}

// ---------- Display label ----------

function getStatusLabel(status: OrderStatus): string {
  switch (status) {
    case "pending":
      return "Awaiting Payment";
    case "paid":
      return "Paid";
    case "shipped":
      return "Shipped";
    case "refunded":
      return "Refunded";
    default:
      return assertNever(status);
  }
}
```

### Optional: a richer label that uses status-specific data

Because the union is discriminated, TypeScript narrows the type in each branch, so you can safely access fields like `trackingNumber` only where they exist:

```typescript
const formatMoney = (cents: Cents) =>
  new Intl.NumberFormat("en-US", { style: "currency", currency: "USD" }).format(cents / 100);

function getOrderDescription(order: Order): string {
  switch (order.status) {
    case "pending":
      return "Awaiting payment";
    case "paid":
      return `Paid on ${order.paidAt.toLocaleDateString()}`;
    case "shipped":
      return `Shipped via ${order.carrier} (tracking: ${order.trackingNumber})`;
    case "refunded":
      return `Refunded ${formatMoney(order.refundAmount)} on ${order.refundedAt.toLocaleDateString()}`;
    default:
      return assertNever(order);
  }
}
```

### Alternative: a lookup map

If you only need simple labels, a `Record` gives the same exhaustiveness guarantee with less code. Omitting a key is a compile error:

```typescript
const STATUS_LABELS: Record<OrderStatus, string> = {
  pending: "Awaiting Payment",
  paid: "Paid",
  shipped: "Shipped",
  refunded: "Refunded",
};

const label = STATUS_LABELS[order.status];
```

### Why this design

- **Impossible states are unrepresentable.** A pending order can't have a `trackingNumber`, and a shipped order must have one.
- **Exhaustiveness checking.** If you add a new status (e.g. `"cancelled"`), every `switch` using `assertNever` and every `Record<OrderStatus, ...>` will fail to compile until you handle it.
- **`OrderStatus` is derived** from the union, so there's a single source of truth.

Here's a type-safe approach using a **discriminated union**, so each status carries only the data relevant to it:

```typescript
// ---- Status-specific shapes ----

interface BaseOrder {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  items: OrderItem[];
  totalCents: number;
  createdAt: Date;
}

interface OrderItem {
  productId: string;
  name: string;
  quantity: number;
  unitPriceCents: number;
}

interface PendingOrder extends BaseOrder {
  status: "pending";
}

interface PaidOrder extends BaseOrder {
  status: "paid";
  paidAt: Date;
  paymentId: string;
}

interface ShippedOrder extends BaseOrder {
  status: "shipped";
  paidAt: Date;
  paymentId: string;
  shippedAt: Date;
  trackingNumber: string;
  carrier: string;
}

interface RefundedOrder extends BaseOrder {
  status: "refunded";
  paidAt: Date;
  paymentId: string;
  refundedAt: Date;
  refundAmountCents: number;
  reason?: string;
}

// ---- The union ----

export type Order = PendingOrder | PaidOrder | ShippedOrder | RefundedOrder;

export type OrderStatus = Order["status"];
// => "pending" | "paid" | "shipped" | "refunded"

// ---- Exhaustiveness helper ----

function assertNever(value: never): never {
  throw new Error(`Unhandled order status: ${JSON.stringify(value)}`);
}

// ---- Display label from a status ----

export function getStatusLabel(status: OrderStatus): string {
  switch (status) {
    case "pending":
      return "Awaiting Payment";
    case "paid":
      return "Paid";
    case "shipped":
      return "Shipped";
    case "refunded":
      return "Refunded";
    default:
      return assertNever(status);
  }
}

// ---- Richer label using status-specific data ----

export function getOrderLabel(order: Order): string {
  switch (order.status) {
    case "pending":
      return "Awaiting Payment";
    case "paid":
      return `Paid on ${order.paidAt.toLocaleDateString()}`;
    case "shipped":
      return `Shipped via ${order.carrier} (${order.trackingNumber})`;
    case "refunded":
      return `Refunded $${(order.refundAmountCents / 100).toFixed(2)}`;
    default:
      return assertNever(order);
  }
}
```

### Alternative: a lookup map

If you only need simple labels, a `Record` is more concise and is still exhaustive, since the compiler errors if a key is missing:

```typescript
export const STATUS_LABELS: Record<OrderStatus, string> = {
  pending: "Awaiting Payment",
  paid: "Paid",
  shipped: "Shipped",
  refunded: "Refunded",
};

const label = STATUS_LABELS[order.status];
```

### Why this design

- **Discriminated union on `status`**: TypeScript narrows the type in each `case`, so `order.trackingNumber` is only accessible when `status === "shipped"`. You can't accidentally read a tracking number off a pending order.
- **Impossible states are unrepresentable**: A shipped order *must* have a tracking number, and a pending order *can't* have one. With a single interface full of optional fields, you would lose that guarantee.
- **`assertNever` for exhaustiveness**: If you later add `"cancelled"` to the union, every `switch` without a matching case becomes a compile error instead of a silent bug.
- **`OrderStatus` is derived** from `Order["status"]`, so there's a single source of truth.
- **Money in cents** (integers) avoids floating-point rounding issues.

