```

Using `Record<OrderStatus, string>` ensures compile-time exhaustiveness—if you add a new status to `OrderStatus` later, TypeScript will error until you add its corresponding label.

Here are the TypeScript types using a discriminated union for the order states, along with a type-safe function to get the display label for each status:

```ts
export type OrderStatus = 'pending' | 'paid' | 'shipped' | 'refunded';

interface BaseOrder {
  id: string;
  amount: number;
  createdAt: Date;
}

export type Order =
  | (BaseOrder & {
      status: 'pending';
    })
  | (BaseOrder & {
      status: 'paid';
      paidAt: Date;
    })
  | (BaseOrder & {
      status: 'shipped';
      paidAt: Date;
      shippedAt: Date;
      trackingNumber: string;
    })
  | (BaseOrder & {
      status: 'refunded';
      refundedAt: Date;
      reason?: string;
    });

const ORDER_STATUS_LABELS: Record<OrderStatus, string> = {
  pending: 'Pending',
  paid: 'Paid',
  shipped: 'Shipped',
  refunded: 'Refunded',
};

export function getOrderStatusLabel(status: OrderStatus): string {
  return ORDER_STATUS_LABELS[status];
}
```

### Why this structure works well
* **Discriminated Union (`Order`)**: Narrowing on `order.status` gives you access to status-specific fields (like `trackingNumber` only when `status === 'shipped'`).
* **Compile-time Exhaustiveness (`Record<OrderStatus, string>`)**: If you add a new status to `OrderStatus` later, TypeScript will immediately error until you add its label to `ORDER_STATUS_LABELS`.

