```typescript
type OrderStatus = "pending" | "paid" | "shipped" | "refunded";

interface Order {
  id: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;       // in cents
  createdAt: Date;
}

function statusLabel(status: OrderStatus): string {
  switch (status) {
    case "pending":  return "Pending";
    case "paid":     return "Paid";
    case "shipped":  return "Shipped";
    case "refunded": return "Refunded";
  }
}
```

Notes:

- The `switch` is exhaustive; if you later add a status to `OrderStatus`, TypeScript will error at the `status` in `statusLabel` (no default case) only if you have `noImplicitReturns` / strict checks — a good way to catch unhandled cases.

A more data-driven alternative:

```typescript
const statusLabels: Record<OrderStatus, string> = {
  pending: "Pending",
  paid: "Paid",
  shipped: "Shipped",
  refunded: "Refunded",
};

const statusLabel = (status: OrderStatus) => statusLabels[status];
```

The `Record<OrderStatus, string>` type forces you to provide a label for every status — adding a new one without a label becomes a compile error automatically.

