const STATUS_LABELS: Record<OrderStatus, string> = {
  pending: "Pending",
  paid: "Paid",
  shipped: "Shipped",
  refunded: "Refunded",
};

function getStatusLabel(order: Order): string {
  return STATUS_LABELS[order.status];
}
```

## Example usage

```typescript
const order: Order = {
  id: "ord-123",
  status: "shipped",
  total: 59.99,
  items: [{ name: "Coffee Mug", quantity: 1, price: 59.99 }],
  createdAt: new Date(),
};

console.log(getStatusLabel(order)); // "Shipped"

// TypeScript catches invalid statuses at compile time:
// order.status = "cancelled"; // ❌ Error: not assignable to OrderStatus
```

## Why this approach?

- **Union type** (`OrderStatus`) restricts status to exactly the four valid values — typos fail at compile time.
- **`Record<OrderStatus, string>`** ensures every status has a label. If you add `"cancelled"` to the union later, TypeScript forces you to add its label too.
- The lookup object avoids a chain of `if/else` or `switch`, and `getStatusLabel` runs in O(1).

If you want richer per-status data (e.g., colors or icons), you could extend the record to `Record<OrderStatus, { label: string; color: string }>` — same pattern, just more fields.

# Order Status Types

```typescript
// Discriminated union for order statuses
type OrderStatus =
  | { status: "pending" }
  | { status: "paid"; paidAt: Date; amount: number }
  | { status: "shipped"; shippedAt: Date; trackingNumber: string }
  | { status: "refunded"; refundedAt: Date; amount: number };

interface Order {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  items: string[];
  createdAt: Date;
  status: OrderStatus;
}

// Exhaustive switch with a `never` check for compile-time safety
function getStatusLabel(order: Order): string {
  switch (order.status.status) {
    case "pending":
      return "Pending";
    case "paid":
      return `Paid $${order.status.amount.toFixed(2)}`;
    case "shipped":
      return `Shipped (Tracking: ${order.status.trackingNumber})`;
    case "refunded":
      return `Refunded $${order.status.amount.toFixed(2)}`;
    default: {
      // If a new status is added, this line fails to compile
      const _exhaustive: never = order.status;
      return _exhaustive;
    }
  }
}
```

## Example usage

```typescript
const order: Order = {
  id: "ord_123",
  customerId: "cust_456",
  items: ["widget", "gadget"],
  createdAt: new Date(),
  status: { status: "shipped", shippedAt: new Date(), trackingNumber: "1Z999AA1" },
};

console.log(getStatusLabel(order)); // "Shipped (Tracking: 1Z999AA1)"
```

## Key points

- **Discriminated union**: the `status` literal acts as a discriminant, so TypeScript narrows to the right variant inside each `case` (e.g., `trackingNumber` is only accessible in the `"shipped"` branch).
- **Exhaustiveness check**: assigning to `never` in the default case ensures the compiler errors if you add a new status variant without handling it here.
- If you don't need extra data per status, a simpler `type OrderStatus = "pending" | "paid" | "shipped" | "refunded"` union works fine too.

