# Traffic Light State Machine in TypeScript

Here's a type-safe implementation using discriminated unions and exhaustive switch checks:

```typescript
// The three possible states
type Red = { readonly state: "red" };
type Yellow = { readonly state: "yellow" };
type Green = { readonly state: "green" };

type TrafficLight = Red | Yellow | Green;

// Convenience constructors
const red: TrafficLight = { state: "red" };
const yellow: TrafficLight = { state: "yellow" };
const green: TrafficLight = { state: "green" };

// Transition function — exhaustively checked by the compiler
function next(current: TrafficLight): TrafficLight {
  switch (current.state) {
    case "red":
      return green;
    case "green":
      return yellow;
    case "yellow":
      return red;
    default: {
      // Compile-time exhaustiveness check:
      // if we ever add a state but forget a case, this errors.
      const _exhaustive: never = current;
      return _exhaustive;
    }
  }
}
```

## Usage

```typescript
