Notes:

- `Record<LogLevel, ...>` guarantees every level has an entry — add a new level to the union and TypeScript will error until you map it.
- `.bind(console)` keeps the `this` context correct (some environments patch `console` methods, and unbound references can throw in strict mode).
- `unknown[]` for args keeps it flexible; use `any[]` if you prefer, or a generic tuple to preserve literal types.

If you need to handle levels that come from outside your code (e.g. a config file or a remote log stream), accept a `string` and fall back:

```ts
export function logSafe(level: string, ...args: unknown[]): void {
  const method = consoleMethods[level as LogLevel];
  if (method) {
    method(...args);
  } else {
    console.log(`[${level}]`, ...args);
  }
}
```

A `switch` is an equally valid alternative if you'd rather avoid the lookup table:

```ts
export function log(level: LogLevel, ...args: unknown[]): void {
  switch (level) {
    case "debug": return console.debug(...args);
    case "info":  return console.info(...args);
    case "warn":  return console.warn(...args);
    case "error": return console.error(...args);
  }
}
```

